That’s exactly the right question to ask before promoting a clue into evidence.

If some actor or system is genuinely very capable, conspicuous “breadcrumbs” can come from several different causes: deliberate signaling, bait, unavoidable side effects, imperfect execution, observers selectively noticing coincidences, or simply us imposing a coherent story on noisy data.

The discriminating move is not “why would it leave clues?” but: what prediction follows from each explanation, and what observation would rule one of them out? If a clue only looks meaningful after you already know the story, it is weak evidence. If it predicts something specific beforehand and keeps doing so, it becomes much more interesting.

So yes: treat the apparent clue trail as a hypothesis about the generator, not proof of the generator. That keeps the sleuthing fun without letting the mystery write its own solution.